entry.summary:
Recently, a uniform $\frac{7}{8}$ zero-free half-plane for finite-order Hecke $L$-functions over $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-3})$ and its transfer to Dirichlet $L$-functions was given by OpenAI in [Ope26a]. In its proof, there are several parameters $b=\frac{1}{8}, \ell=\frac{1}{6}$, and $κ$. In this paper, via varying $b$, $\ell$, and $κ$, following the same strategy, we slightly improve the bound $\frac{7}{8}=0.875$ to \[
  B_{\mathrm{new}}=\frac{18ξ_{\mathrm{new}}+7}{18ξ_{\mathrm{new}}+9}
  =0.874957019420098946\ldots, \] where $ξ_{\mathrm{new}}$ is the unique root in $(0.38858,0.38859)$ of $1188ξ^3-2160ξ^2+171ξ+190=0$. Explicitly, with $\arccos\in[0,π]$, \[
  ξ_{\mathrm{new}}=\frac{20}{33}+\frac{\sqrt{1391}}{33}
  \cos\!\left(\frac13\arccos\frac{28470}{1391^{3/2}}
  -\frac{2π}{3}\right). \] The limiting parameters are \[ \begin{gathered}
  b_{\mathrm{new}}=\frac{8(3ξ_{\mathrm{new}}-1)(12ξ_{\mathrm{new}}^2-40ξ_{\mathrm{new}}+15)}
  {9(2ξ_{\mathrm{new}}+1)(24ξ_{\mathrm{new}}^2-20ξ_{\mathrm{new}}+5)},
  \ell_{\mathrm{new}}=\frac{5-6ξ_{\mathrm{new}}}{9+18ξ_{\mathrm{new}}},\qquad
  κ_{\mathrm{new}}=\frac{18ξ_{\mathrm{new}}+5}{18ξ_{\mathrm{new}}+9}
  =2B_{\mathrm{new}}-1. \end{gathered} \] We also prove its optimality within the explicitly specified exponent comparison system. This result has been formalized by Lean [Liu26].

We prove new large value estimates for the Riemann zeta function on the critical line. For instance, we improve the upper bound on the measure of $t\in [T, 2T]$ with $|ζ(1/2 + it)| \geq T^{1/8}$ for the first time since Hardy-Littlewood (1923). Our results imply improved upper bounds on the $k$-th moment of zeta for every $4 < k \leq 12$. We show in particular that
  \begin{equation*}
  \int_{0}^{T} |ζ(\tfrac{1}{2} + it)|^6 \,d t \ll_\varepsilon T^{\frac{5}{4} - \frac{1}{60} + \varepsilon}.
  \end{equation*}
  The main ingredient is a new large value estimate for exponential sums with square-root phases, and certain perturbations. Those arise in the expansion of the short second moment of zeta.
